Public fair value is 5 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 12.0. The informed trader knows the exact sum S = 5 + d2 + d3, which is symmetric around 12. Noise traders value the contract at about N(12, 4.4).

Quoting an ask A, the expected edge is 0.35 × E[A − S; S > A] + 0.65 × P(V > A) × (A − 12). This gives about +0.07 at A = 13, +0.33 at A = 14, +0.44 at A = 15, +0.46 at A = 16, and +0.42 at A = 17. The best ask is 16.0. By symmetry the best bid is 8.0. Position is flat, so no skew is needed.

{"bid": 8.0, "ask": 16.0}

Die 3 is still unknown, so the public fair value is 9 + 3.5 = 12.5, and the informed trader knows the sum S ∈ {10,…,15}. Position is flat, so I don't need to skew for inventory.

Taking the expected edge on each side (noise traders trade only when their valuation is beyond my quote, and informed traders trade only when S is beyond it), the ask edge peaks near 16 and the bid edge is the mirror image around 12.5, peaking near 9. A bid of 9 and an ask of 16 are each about 0.48 in expected edge against this flow, and the quotes are symmetric.

{"bid": 9.0, "ask": 16.0}

Die 1 is revealed as 1, so the public fair value is 1 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 8. Die 2 is not revealed until before stage 3, so the unknown part is die 2 plus die 3 (mean 7).

Quoting around the fair value of 8 with a spread that compensates for adverse selection from informed traders, I get roughly 5.0 on the bid and 11.0 on the ask. The distribution of the settlement is symmetric around 8, so the two sides are symmetric. The one-sided expected profit is maximized near a bid of 5.

{"bid": 5.0, "ask": 11.0}

Fair value is 4 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 11.0, since die 2 and die 3 are still unknown. Quotes 1–3 traded only on the ask side, so the market has been leaning toward buying from us. Our net position is -1, so we want to buy back a little.

I set a wide two-sided quote because a tight spread loses money to informed traders. Their trades are fully adverse, and the noise-trader edge doesn't cover that near fair value. The rough per-quote expectations I checked put the best spread near fair ± 2.5 to 3. I skew the quote slightly upward (bid 8.8, ask 13.5) to make it easier to buy back the short.

{"bid": 8.8, "ask": 13.5}

With dice 1 and 2 revealed as 4 and 4, the settlement value is 8 + die 3, which is uniform on {9,...,14} with mean 11.5. Noise traders value the contract around 11.5 with standard deviation 3, and informed traders know the exact value.

Quoting a one-sided edge per side (roughly, the spread earned on noise flow minus adverse selection from informed flow) peaks near an ask of 14.0 and a bid of 9.0 to 9.5. I'm short 1, so I'll lean the bid up slightly to 9.5 to make a buy-back more likely, and keep the ask at 14.0 so I don't add to the short.

{"bid": 9.5, "ask": 14.0}

Nothing is revealed yet, so the public fair value is the expected sum of three fair dice, 10.5. My position is flat, so there's no inventory skew to apply.

Against a 20% informed flow, a rough one-sided edge calculation peaks near 14 on the ask. By symmetry around 10.5, the bid peaks near 7. Both are close to the last quote, so I'll keep the same two-sided quote.

{"bid": 7, "ask": 14}

Die 1 is 3, and dice 2 and 3 are unknown. The public fair value is 3 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 10, and the settlement is symmetric around 10. Noise traders buy or sell with valuations around 10 ± 4.4, while the informed trader's settlement value S ranges from 5 to 15.

The previous 7/14 quotes were never hit, so they were too wide to earn anything. I chose quotes that maximise expected profit against this mix of noise and informed flow. The ask balances the noise-trader profit (ask − 10) against the probability of a noise buy, minus the expected loss to the informed trader. That balance falls near 13.6. The bid mirrors it around 10 at 6.4. Checking nearby prices, 13.5 and 14 both give slightly lower expected profit than 13.6.

{"bid": 6.4, "ask": 13.6}

The public fair value is 3 + 4 + 3.5 = 10.5, since die 3 is unrevealed and has mean 3.5. The informed counterparty knows the sum S, which is uniform on {8, ..., 13}. With no future quotes left, each side maximises its expected profit separately.

For the ask, the expected profit is 0.2·E[(A−S)·1{S>A}] + 0.8·P(V>A)·(A−10.5), where V ~ N(10.5, 4.4) is the noise valuation. The derivative of this is roughly zero near A ≈ 13.8. The bid is symmetric about 10.5 because S is symmetric, so the optimum is about 10.5 − 3.3 = 7.2. Both values are consistent with the prior quotes.

{"bid": 7.2, "ask": 13.8}

**Fair value:** Die 1 = 5 is known, and dice 2 and 3 are unknown with expected value 3.5 each, so the public fair value is 5 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 12.0. Die 2 is not revealed until before stage 3.

**Pricing:** Noise buyers arrive with valuations around 12 (SD 4.4), and informed traders only trade against quotes that are wrong in their direction. Quoting wide captures most of the margin from noise flow while losing little to informed flow, which is rare at extreme prices. My rough calculation gives expected profit per side of about 0.57 at a 6-point distance from fair value. That is flat over ask 15 to 16 and bid 8 to 9, and clearly better than the tighter 10.5/12.5 quote from last stage. The position of +1 doesn't change the expected-profit comparison in a risk-neutral setting, so I'm not skewing the quote.

{"bid": 9, "ask": 15}

The fair value has moved down to about 9.9. Quote 1 (no trade at 8/13) and quote 2 (they sold to us at 8) are both informative. An informed seller at 8 means the sum is at most 7, so the sale is evidence of a low settlement value. Conditioning on both events gives a posterior mean of about 9.9 and a standard deviation of about 3.

With that centre, I balanced the expected noise-trader edge against the informed-trader adverse selection on each side. That puts the ask near 12.5 to 12.6 and the bid near 7.1 to 7.2. I'm quoting 7.1 and 12.6, which brackets the 9.9 centre.

{"bid": 7.1, "ask": 12.6}

**Fair value:** Die 1 = 6 is known. The remaining dice have mean 3.5 each, so the naive value is 13. The no-trade results at quotes 1 and 3 are informative, though. An informed counterparty would have traded if the sum were above 13 (quote 1) or above 12.6 (quote 3), so those non-trades tilt the posterior toward lower sums. Quote 2 was a noise-trader sale and carries no information about the dice. Updating on the history gives a public fair value of about 12.35.

**Quote:** With 20% informed flow, the expected profit at each side is maximised close to fair value. Maximising the expected profit on each side gives an ask of about 14.9 and a bid of about 9.9. Position +1 doesn't change this, since I'm risk-neutral and the fair value is a martingale.

{"bid": 9.9, "ask": 14.9}

Die 1 is 6 and the other two dice are unrevealed, so the public fair value is 6 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 13. The position is flat, so there is no inventory skew.

Quoting against a 20% informed flow, the adverse-selection cost is balanced against the noise-trader spread. This puts the ask near 15.6 and, by the symmetry of the sum around 13, the bid near 10.4.

{"bid": 10.4, "ask": 15.6}

At quote 7, die 1 = 6 and die 2 = 2 are known, so the fair value is 6 + 2 + 3.5 = 11.5. The informed counterparty knows the sum, which is uniform on 9 to 14. Noise traders value the contract at N(11.5, 3).

For each side, I computed expected profit from the noise and informed flow, weighted 0.8 and 0.2. The ask at 14 and the bid at 9 both give about 0.40 per quote, and moving either side closer to fair value lowers the expected profit because of adverse selection from the informed trader. Position is flat, so inventory is not a concern yet.

{"bid": 9, "ask": 14}

Before any reveal, both sides have the same public information. The prior mean is 6.75 (die 1) + 6.8 (die 2) + 4.875 (die 3) = 18.425, with standard deviation about 6.5.

Informed traders can pick off any quote near 18.4, so I quote wide. A normal approximation of the trade-off between noise-trader profit and informed-trader losses peaks at roughly 7.5 away from fair value. That puts the ask near 26.0 and the bid near 10.9, after rounding.

{"bid": 10.9, "ask": 26.0}

The public fair value is the sum of the die means: 6.75 (die 1) + 6.8 (die 2) + 4.875 (die 3) = 18.425. Nothing is revealed yet, so the settlement standard deviation is about 6.5.

With a symmetric half-spread h around 18.425, the informed trader's expected adverse-selection loss roughly offsets the noise-trader gain near h ≈ 4. The expected profit per quote peaks around h ≈ 7.5–8, where it is about +0.5. A half-spread of 8 gives a bid near 10.4 and an ask near 26.5, which is close to the previous quote's centre and width.

{"bid": 10.4, "ask": 26.5}

The public fair value is the sum of the expected dice: 6.75 + 6.8 + 4.875 = 18.425. The total standard deviation is about 6.5, the same as the noise traders' spread.

With a 50% informed share, the optimal half-spread around fair value solves 1 − Φ(t) = 0.5·t·φ(t) with t = s/σ, which gives t ≈ 1.19 and s ≈ 7.7. That puts the quotes near 10.7 and 26.1, close to the previous quotes. No trades so far only mildly truncates the distribution, so I'm keeping the quotes roughly where they were.

{"bid": 10.7, "ask": 26.1}

Die 2 is still hidden, so the public fair value is the expected sum of the known and unknown parts: die 1 averages 6.75, die 2 averages 6.8, and die 3 is 1. That gives about 14.55.

Quotes are set where the marginal gain from a noise trader equals the marginal loss to informed traders. For the ask, the informed buyers' distribution has a jump at 22 (about 12.5% of the probability sits at 21 to 22 and only 5% above 22), so 22.0 is the best ask. For the bid, the optimum is flat around 7.0 to 7.1. Holding +1 doesn't change the expected settlement profit, so I'm not skewing for inventory.

{"bid": 7.0, "ask": 22.0}

Fair value with nothing revealed is the mean of the sum: 3.75 + 2.5 + 6.25 = 12.5. The variance is about 33.6, so the standard deviation is about 5.8, matching the noise traders' spread.

Against informed traders, a symmetric spread around 12.5 is costly. Treating the sum as roughly normal, the myopic optimum half-spread is about 1.2 standard deviations, or roughly 7. The previous quotes (5.9 / 19.6) sit close to that. I'll keep the ask at 19.6. We are short one unit, so I'll nudge the bid up slightly to make a buy-back fill more likely.

{"bid": 6.2, "ask": 19.6}

Public fair value is 10 + 2.5 (die 2 mean) + 6.25 (die 3 mean) = 18.75. Die 2 stays hidden and die 3 is revealed only before stage 3, so this quote uses 18.75 as the fair value.

Balancing adverse selection from informed traders against noise-trader flow (sd 5.8) gives a bid of about 12.0 and an ask of about 24.8. Inventory at -1 doesn't change these optima, since the objective is expected settlement profit. The ask is close to flat between 24.8 and 24.9, so 24.8 is essentially equivalent to 24.9.

{"bid": 12.0, "ask": 24.8}

**Fair value:** Die 1 has mean 6.75 and die 2 (unrevealed) has mean 0.3·1 + 0.1·7 + 0.4·9 + 0.2·11 = 6.8. With die 3 = 3, the public fair value is 16.55.

**Quote:** Bid is pulled at +3, so I only quote an ask. The counterparty is informed with probability 0.5, and an informed buyer only trades when the true sum exceeds my ask, so that trade costs me E[(V−A)⁺]. A noise buyer trades when its valuation (16.55 + N(0, 6.5²)) exceeds my ask, which earns me A − 16.55 per trade. Maximising the expected profit 0.5·[−E[(V−A)⁺] + P(N > A)(A − 16.55)] over the exact distribution of the sum gives a best ask of about 24.0 (value ≈ +0.43 per quote). Asks of 23.8 and 24.1 are slightly worse.

{"bid": null, "ask": 24.0}

Under the public information, the expected sum is 19.6. Die 1 averages 7, die 2 averages 4.6, and die 3 averages 8. The total standard deviation is about 3.6.

With no inventory yet, I quote symmetrically around 19.6. A rough calculation of expected profit per side suggests a half-spread of about 3.5, which balances noise-trader profit against losses to informed traders. That gives a bid of 16.1 and an ask of 23.1.

{"bid": 16.1, "ask": 23.1}

Die 2 is revealed as 6, and die 1 is 8. Die 3 has mean 8, so the public fair value is 8 + 6 + 8 = **22**. Settlement can only be 21, 22, or 24, which means an informed counterparty never buys above 24 or sells below 21.

Quoting bid 19.2 and ask 24.8 keeps both sides at about 0.4 expected profit per noise trade. A 24.8 ask is about 2.8 above fair, which trades off the chance of a noise buy against the margin earned. Pricing outside 21–24 also means the informed trader never trades against me on either side.

{"bid": 19.2, "ask": 24.8}

Expected sum: die 1 averages 5.75, die 2 averages 5, die 3 averages 5.25, so the public fair value is 16.0. The sum's standard deviation is about 6.6, close to the noise traders' 6.6.

With nothing revealed, the informed traders are the main risk. Setting bid and ask symmetric at distance d from 16, the expected profit is roughly 2d·Φ(−d/σ) − 0.4σ·φ(d/σ) with σ = 6.6. Maximising this gives d ≈ 0.875σ ≈ 5.8, so I quote about 10.2 bid and 21.8 ask. This is a normal approximation, so treat the exact numbers as approximate.

{"bid": 10.2, "ask": 21.8}

With no information yet, the fair value is 16: die 1 averages 5.75, die 2 averages 5, and die 3 averages 5.25. The sum's standard deviation is about 6.6.

Against 20% informed flow, the one-shot optimum for a symmetric quote is roughly ±5.8 around 16. That solves 1 − Φ(z) = 0.8·z·φ(z) at z ≈ 0.88 with σ ≈ 6.6. This is a myopic result, so it's a starting point rather than a full dynamic solution.

{"bid": 10.2, "ask": 21.8}

Fair value is the expected sum: 5.75 (die 1) + 5 (die 2) + 5.25 (die 3) = 16. Nothing is revealed yet, so the public fair value is 16.

With probability 0.2 the counterparty knows the settlement value and trades only when the quote is wrong for it. Balancing that against the noise-trader edge, the optimal half-spread is about 0.875 × 6.6 ≈ 5.8. That puts the bid near 10.2 and the ask near 21.8, which matches quote 1. Quote 2 at 10/22 was slightly wider than that, so I'll return to 10.2/21.8.

{"bid": 10.2, "ask": 21.8}

Fair value for the settlement is 11 + 8 + E[die 3] = 19 + 8 = 27. The informed trader knows the sum, which is 26, 27 or 29, with probabilities 1/2, 1/4 and 1/4.

Quoting outside the informed trader's possible values avoids adverse selection. A bid at or below 26 is never hit by an informed seller, and an ask at or above 29 is never lifted by an informed buyer. Within that range, noise-trader flow is the only source of fills. Since the noise valuation is N(27, 3.6), the spread that maximises expected edge per quote sits about 0.75 standard deviations (about 2.7) from 27 on each side. That gives roughly 24.3 for the bid and 29.7 for the ask. Expected profit is linear, so the short position of -3 doesn't change the optimal prices. Both sides are well inside the ±4 limits.

{"bid": 24.3, "ask": 29.7}

Die 2 is known to be 8 and die 1 is 11, so settlement is 19 + die 3. Die 3's mean is 8, so the fair value is 27. Its outcomes give 26 (prob 1/2), 27 (1/4), or 29 (1/4).

I'm short 4 and can only bid, so a fill means buying back one unit. A trade gains me (V − bid) at settlement. The informed counterparty only sells to me when V is below my bid, so any bid at or below 26 avoids adverse informed fills. Above 26, informed sellers add losses. Among noise-trader sales, the bid that maximises expected profit is about 27 − 0.75 × 3.6 ≈ 24.3. The bid is already a multiple of 0.1, so no rounding is needed.

{"bid": 24.3, "ask": null}

The expected settlement value is the sum of the die means: 8 + 5.33 + 6.5 = 19.83. The standard deviation of the total is about 5.7, roughly the same as the noise traders' spread. Informed traders (35%) will pick off stale quotes, so I'll quote a half-spread of about 5, somewhat wider than the previous quote's 4.4 to account for that.

Bid: 19.83 − 5.0 ≈ 14.8. Ask: 19.83 + 5.0 ≈ 24.9.

{"bid": 14.8, "ask": 24.9}

Die 2 is revealed as 6, so the settlement value is 6, 9, 16 or 17, each with probability 1/4. The public fair value is 12.

A noise trader (about 4.7 standard deviation around 12) trades with us when our quote is mispriced relative to 12. An informed trader trades only when the bid is above the true value (V = 6 or 9) or the ask is below it (V = 16 or 17). Informed trades lose money for us, so the bid should sit below 9 and the ask below 17 to limit that exposure. Balancing the noise-trader edge against informed losses gives a bid near 7.6 and an ask near 16.2. Both are one-period optima and ignore the effect on the final quote.

{"bid": 7.6, "ask": 16.2}

Fair value is 14: die 1 has mean 6 and die 2 has mean 8, and nothing is revealed yet. The settlement sum has standard deviation about 6.3, which is larger than the noise traders' 4.7, so informed flow is costly and the quotes should be wide.

Checking the expected per-quote profit on each side (noise-trader gain minus informed-trader loss) over the exact 24-outcome distribution, the ask is best near 18 and the bid is best near 10. Being short one unit would argue for a slightly higher bid, but the effect is small, so I keep the bid at 10.0.

{"bid": 10.0, "ask": 18.0}

Expected settlement value with no information: die 1 averages 8, die 2 averages 5.33, die 3 averages 6.5, so the sum has mean ≈ 19.83 and standard deviation ≈ 5.73. The sum is approximately normal, so I'm using a normal approximation.

Against this prior, the expected profit per side is roughly flat across asks from about 25 to 26, at about 0.42 per side. A tighter quote loses to informed flow. By symmetry the bid sits about the same distance below 19.83, around 14.1. This is close to the previous quote, which was 14.3 / 25.4.

{"bid": 14.1, "ask": 25.6}

Fair value is now about 22.3. Die 1 has mean 8, die 2 has mean 5.33, and die 3 is revealed as 9. Only die 1 and die 2 remain uncertain, with combined standard deviation about 4.4. That is tighter than the noise traders' 5.7, so informed flow is the main risk.

Balancing expected profit from noise traders against adverse selection from informed traders, the best half-spread is about 5 on each side, which gives a bid near 17.3 and an ask near 27.3. Our position is flat, so no inventory skew is needed.

{"bid": 17.3, "ask": 27.3}

The public fair value is about 22.3 before any trade information. Die 3 is 9, and the expected sum of die 1 (mean 8) and die 2 (mean 5.33) gives 22.33. The earlier no-trade quotes at asks of 25.4 and 25.6 pull the estimate down slightly, and the 27.3 buy pushes it up slightly, so I take the fair value as about 22.6.

With the 35% informed flow, a half-spread of roughly 4.5 to 5 around fair value balances adverse selection against noise-trader flow. I'm short one unit, so I shade the mid up a little to attract sellers to my bid and discourage further buyers at my ask. That gives a bid of 18.2 and an ask of 27.4.

{"bid": 18.2, "ask": 27.4}

Die 1 has mean 5 (its faces sum to 40 over 8), so with die 2 = 10 the public fair value is 15. The informed counterparty knows the settlement value V = die 1 + 10, which can only be 12, 13, 14, 16, 17 or 18.

With the bid at 11.5 and the ask at 18.5, no value of V triggers an informed trade. Only noise traders can hit either side, and each sells or buys at a price that is about 3.5 away from the 15 fair value. That gives an expected edge of roughly 0.4 per quote, and moving either quote closer to 15 does worse.

{"bid": 11.5, "ask": 18.5}

Public fair value is 15: die 1 averages 5, and die 2 is revealed as 10. The informed counterparty knows the true value, which is 12 to 18 given die 2 = 10. Since this is the last quote, I only need to maximise expected profit from this one trade and inventory is zero.

Choosing the ask to maximise 0.5·[−E(V−A)⁺ + (A−15)·P(X>A)], where X is the noise valuation, gives an optimum near A ≈ 18.5. At that level no informed trader buys, because V ≤ 18, so the ask earns only from noise traders. The symmetric calculation for the bid gives B ≈ 11.5, rounded down to 11.4, where no informed trader sells because V ≥ 12.

{"bid": 11.4, "ask": 18.5}

Expected sum from the public information: die 1 averages 6.33, die 2 averages 6.25, and die 3 is revealed as 5, so the fair value is about 17.58. Die 2 is still hidden, so I'm using its prior mean.

Against a noise trader I earn the distance from fair value on each trade. Against an informed trader I lose the amount by which the true sum overshoots my ask, or undershoots my bid. Noise sd (9.8) is much larger than the sum's sd (about 5.85), so informed flow dominates near the mean. Balancing those two effects gives an optimal offset of roughly 9.5 from fair on each side: ask about 27.1, bid about 8.0. Inventory doesn't change expected profit in this model, so the +3 position doesn't shift the quotes.

{"bid": 8.0, "ask": 27.1}

Fair value is 13.25: die 3 = 5, die 1 = 2, and die 2 averages 6.25. The informed trader knows the sum, which is 8, 9, or 18 (18 with probability 1/2). An ask at or above 18 means the informed trader never buys, so any buy at that price comes from a noise trader and carries no information about the sum.

Being long 4, I want to sell. Expected profit from an ask a is roughly 0.5·P(V > a)·(a − 13.25) for noise traders, plus an adverse-selection term that only applies below 18. Above 18 the noise-trader term alone is maximised near a ≈ 20.6, and 20.6 edges out 20.7 slightly. The bid is pulled at +4, so I quote the ask only.

{"bid": null, "ask": 20.6}

The sum's expected value is 18.92: die 1 averages 6.33, die 2 averages 6.25, and die 3 averages 6.33. The sum's standard deviation is about 6.5. Noise traders' valuations spread over ±9.8, which is wide relative to the information, so the main risk is informed traders picking off a tight quote. Balancing noise-trader spread capture against adverse selection gives a half-spread of roughly 9.4 around the fair value.

Bid 9.5 and ask 28.4, rounded to the 0.1 tick (bid down, ask up).

{"bid": 9.5, "ask": 28.4}

Expected sum: die 1 averages 6.33, die 2 averages 6.25, die 3 averages 6.33, so the fair value is about 18.9 with standard deviation about 6.5. Noise traders value the contract at fair value plus noise with standard deviation 9.8, so most counterparties are uninformed.

Setting expected profit per trade to zero on each side (informed traders pick off stale prices, noise traders don't) gives break-even levels of roughly 22.3 for the ask and 15.4 for the bid. I'm quoting at those levels, so expected profit per fill is about zero and the spread is roughly 7. This is a rough normal approximation to the discrete distribution.

{"bid": 15.4, "ask": 22.3}

The public fair value is 21: each card has mean 7, so three cards sum to 21 on average. The sum's standard deviation is about 6.35, close to the noise traders' 6.4.

With no information revealed yet, I quote symmetrically around 21. The main risk is that the informed half of counterparties trade only when the settlement value is far from my quote. A rough calculation of expected profit per side, weighing noise-trader spread capture against informed-trader losses, peaks at a half-spread of about 7 to 8. I'll use 7.5.

{"bid": 13.5, "ask": 28.5}

Card 1 is 4, so the remaining 51 cards average 360/51 ≈ 7.06. Two unknown cards remain, so the public fair value is about 4 + 2(7.06) ≈ 18.1.

I'm long 3 units, so I want to avoid buying more and I'd like to sell. I'll set the bid below fair value to discourage further buying and the ask close to fair value to attract buyers. The quote is 15.0 / 18.3, a spread of 3.3 centred about 1.5 below fair value.

{"bid": 15.0, "ask": 18.3}

Cards 1 and 2 are revealed as 12 and 4, so the fair value of the settlement is 16 + E[card 3]. The remaining 50 cards have values summing to 348, so E[card 3] = 6.96 and the public fair value is about 22.96.

The informed counterparty's valuation is 16 + card 3, which ranges from 17 to 29. I estimated expected profit on each side across candidate prices, weighting noise traders (valuation ~ N(22.96, 4.8²), probability 0.8) and informed traders (probability 0.2). The ask side peaks near 27 (about 0.60). The bid side peaks near 19 (about 0.60). Position is 0 and there is only one quote after this one, so inventory considerations are minor.

{"bid": 19.0, "ask": 27.0}

Card 1 is 13, so the remaining two cards average (351/51) ≈ 6.88 each. The public fair value is 13 + 2 × 6.88 ≈ 26.77, and the sum's standard deviation is about 5.2.

Quoting at fair value loses money to informed traders, who pick off a mid-priced quote. A one-period model (20% informed, 80% noise with sd 4.8) puts the best expected profit at about 4.3 away from fair value on each side. That gives a bid near 22.5 and an ask near 31.1. Prices round to 0.1, so the bid rounds down to 22.4 and the ask rounds up to 31.1. The current short position doesn't change expected profit, so I'm not skewing the quote for it.

{"bid": 22.4, "ask": 31.1}

Fair value is 30.8: the remaining 50 cards sum to 340, so card 3 averages 6.8 and 13 + 11 + 6.8 = 30.8. Since card 3 stays hidden until settlement, the fair value won't move before quote 6.

I'm short 2, so I want to skew quotes slightly upward to encourage buying back inventory. I'll keep a spread around fair value to cover the 20% informed-trader risk.

{"bid": 30.8, "ask": 32.9}

Stage 1 reveals nothing, so the public fair value is the expected sum of three cards: 3 × 7 = 21. The standard deviation of the sum without replacement is about 6.35.

I compared half-spreads around 21 by expected profit per side, using a noise-trader fill probability of 0.65 × P(noise > half-spread) and an informed-trader loss of 0.35 × E[(V − ask)⁺]. The profit peaks at a half-spread of about 5.5, which gives 15.5 bid and 26.5 ask. I'm keeping the same quote as last time, since the position is flat and nothing new has been revealed.

