Fair value is 10 + 2 × (354/51) ≈ 23.88, since the two unrevealed cards are drawn from the remaining 51 cards, which sum to 354. Our position is flat, so there's no inventory skew.

With a 35% informed share, each side's quote trades off noise-trader volume against adverse selection. Modelling the true value as roughly N(23.9, 5.2²) and noise valuations as N(23.9, 4.8²), the profit-maximising half-spread is about 5. That gives a bid near 18.9 and an ask near 28.9. Bids round down, so the bid is 18.8.

{"bid": 18.8, "ask": 28.9}

Fair value: card 1 is 2. The remaining 51 cards sum to 362, so each unseen card averages 362/51 ≈ 7.10. Cards 2 and 3 together add about 14.2, so fair value ≈ 16.2.

The last trade was a buy at 19.2, so the buyer's valuation was above 19.2. That nudges my estimate up slightly. I'm short 1, so I want to buy back: I'll raise the bid to attract sellers, and keep the ask high enough that I don't sell more cheaply. I'll quote a spread of about ±2.5 to 3 around ~16.9 to cover adverse selection from the 35% informed flow.

